<?xml version="1.0" ?>

<!-- ============================================================================================
     WATER baseline for the 2D near-wellbore problem, on the plain single-phase phase-field
     poromechanics stack.

     This is the AS wellbore with the chemistry taken out, kept deliberately close to
     AS_wellbore_eigenstrain.xml so the two are comparable: same mesh, same notches, same symmetry
     constraints, same outlet, same injection interval and duration. What differs is only the
     physics that the AS case adds.

     SOLVER STACK, and why it is simpler than the AS one:
       PhaseFieldPoromechanics  ->  SinglePhasePoromechanics (damageFlag=1)
                                      ->  SolidMechanicsLagrangianFEM
                                      ->  SinglePhaseFVM  (TwoPointFluxApproximation)
                                ->  PhaseFieldDamageFEM
     No reactive transport, so no species rows, no K(T), no prescribed temperature field, and no
     clogging permeability. A standard MGR strategy exists for SinglePhasePoromechanics, which the
     reactive solver has none of, so the linear solve is both cheaper and better conditioned.

     TPFA, not hybrid mimetic. inputFiles/phaseField/PhaseFieldPoromechanics_Nucleation_Wellbore.xml
     runs SinglePhaseHybridFVM on a HybridMimeticDiscretization; this deck uses SinglePhaseFVM on a
     TwoPointFluxApproximation instead, matching the discretization the AS decks are built on so the
     flow solution is directly comparable.

     fracturePressureTermFlag = 0: the fluid pressure inside the crack does NOT contribute to the
     damage driving force. Damage is then driven purely by the degraded elastic energy from the
     poroelastic stress state. This makes the damage problem materially easier - the pressure term
     couples the damage residual back to flow and is a common source of the sequential loop failing
     to settle - at the cost of under-driving the crack relative to a full hydraulic model. Turn it
     back on once the case is known to run.

     Granite as in the AS deck (2-2446, Table 3): E = 40 GPa, nu = 0.23, Gc = 12 J/m2, L = 0.5 mm,
     phi = 0.05. SI throughout.

     INJECTION: 5 mL/min for 200 s into a 50 mm interval; this 1 mm slice takes 1/50 of it. At the
     water density 1000 kg/m3 that is (5e-6/60)*(1/50)*1000 = 1.6667e-6 kg/s - lower than the AS
     deck's 2.075e-6 kg/s purely because water is lighter than saturated AS, same volumetric rate.

     EXPECTED RESULT, so it is not mistaken for a failure: at bulkPermeability = 5e-16 the steady
     borehole pressure is ~1.3 MPa, well below the ~3.8 MPa needed to drive the notched crack, so
     this case should NOT fracture. That is the point of a baseline - in the AS case clogging is
     what raises the pressure to breakdown. To get a fracturing water case for testing the damage
     machinery on its own, drop bulkPermeability toward the true granite value (1e-18 gives 655 MPa
     and fractures within ~1 s); 1e-17 is a reasonable middle.
     ============================================================================================ -->
<Problem>

  <Solvers
    gravityVector="{ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }">

    <PhaseFieldPoromechanics
      name="pfSolve"
      poromechanicsSolverName="poroSolve"
      damageSolverName="damageSolve"
      logLevel="1"
      initialDt="1e-3"
      targetRegions="{ Source, Rock }">
      <NonlinearSolverParameters
        couplingType="Sequential"
        lineSearchAction="None"
        newtonMaxIter="20"/>
    </PhaseFieldPoromechanics>

    <!-- damageFlag = 1 selects the SinglePhasePoromechanicsDamageKernel, which is what makes the
         poromechanics aware of the damage field. It is required whenever the solid is a
         PorousDamage* type; without it the assembly takes the undamaged path and the crack never
         affects the stress. -->
    <SinglePhasePoromechanics
      name="poroSolve"
      solidSolverName="lagsolve"
      flowSolverName="singlePhaseFlow"
      logLevel="1"
      targetRegions="{ Source, Rock }"
      damageFlag="0">
      <!-- couplingType MUST be written out. Left unset, this solver behaves as Sequential despite
           the FullyImplicit default, and the flow sub-solver then dies in HypreVector::set on a
           vector that was never allocated. MGR also preconditions the assembled
           displacement/pressure system, so it only applies in the monolithic case. The outer
           phase-field loop stays Sequential; only this level is monolithic.

           damageFlag = 0 is required here, and it costs nothing this deck wants. Setting it to 1
           selects SinglePhasePoromechanicsDamage, whose only addition is computeFractureFlowTerm -
           the body force from fluid flowing ALONG the crack - and which reads the "pressureGradient"
           field. That field is registered solely by SinglePhaseHybridFVM ("pressureGradient is
           specific for HybridFVM", SinglePhaseHybridFVM.cpp:76), so damageFlag=1 cannot run on
           TPFA at all. Dropping it removes precisely the fluid-pressure-drives-the-crack coupling
           that fracturePressureTermFlag=0 also switches off, so the two are consistent.

           Damage still degrades the stiffness: that happens inside PorousDamageSolid /
           DamageSpectralElasticIsotropic, and PorousDamageSolid is in the regular (non-damage)
           kernel dispatch list, so the constitutive degradation is unaffected. -->
      <NonlinearSolverParameters
        couplingType="FullyImplicit"
        lineSearchAction="None"
        newtonMaxIter="40"
        newtonTol="1.0e-4"
        maxTimeStepCuts="8"
        maxSubSteps="200"/>
      <LinearSolverParameters
        solverType="gmres"
        preconditionerType="mgr"
        krylovTol="1.0e-6"/>
    </SinglePhasePoromechanics>

    <SolidMechanicsLagrangianFEM
      name="lagsolve"
      logLevel="1"
      discretization="FE1"
      targetRegions="{ Source, Rock }">
      <NonlinearSolverParameters
        newtonTol="1.0e-4"
        newtonMaxIter="40"/>
      <LinearSolverParameters
        solverType="gmres"
        preconditionerType="amg"
        krylovTol="1.0e-6"/>
    </SolidMechanicsLagrangianFEM>

    <!-- allowNegativePressure: dilation around the pressurizing borehole pulls a few cells to a
         small suction, ordinary for a saturated porous solid; the default check rejects any
         negative pressure and cuts dt indefinitely. -->
    <SinglePhaseFVM
      name="singlePhaseFlow"
      discretization="singlePhaseTPFA"
      targetRegions="{ Source, Rock }"
      allowNegativePressure="1"
      logLevel="1">
      <NonlinearSolverParameters
        newtonTol="1.0e-6"
        maxTimeStepCuts="8"
        maxSubSteps="200"
        newtonMaxIter="30"
        lineSearchAction="None"/>
      <LinearSolverParameters
        solverType="gmres"
        preconditionerType="amg"
        krylovTol="1.0e-6"/>
    </SinglePhaseFVM>

    <!-- fracturePressureTermFlag = 0 (also the code default) - see the header.
         damageUpperBound must be <= 1: the default 1.5 makes irreversibility inert, since damage
         saturates at 1 and the constraint only fires at d >= damageUpperBound.
         viscousRegularizationCoeff = 1.0 Pa.s adds eta/(Gc*dt) to the Jacobian diagonal
         (Miehe 2010, CMAME Eq. 48), keeping it definite. The term scales as 1/dt, so recheck its
         size if dt grows much. -->
    <PhaseFieldDamageFEM
      name="damageSolve"
      discretization="FE1"
      timeIntegrationOption="SteadyState"
      irreversibilityFlag="1"
      damageUpperBound="1.0"
      fracturePressureTermFlag="0"
      viscousRegularizationCoeff="1.0"
      logLevel="1"
      targetRegions="{ Source, Rock }">
      <NonlinearSolverParameters
        newtonMaxIter="200"
        newtonTol="1.0e-6"/>
      <LinearSolverParameters
        solverType="gmres"
        preconditionerType="amg"
        krylovMaxIter="200"
        krylovTol="1.0e-6"/>
    </PhaseFieldDamageFEM>
  </Solvers>

  <!-- Bounds are +/-0.075 m in x and y, +/-0.5 mm in z: already in metres, so no scale attribute. -->
  <Mesh>
    <VTKMesh
      name="mesh"
      file="../../../../../runs/ammoniumSulfate/wellbore/meshes/wellboreFill2DMeterBased.vtu"
      regionAttribute="SOURCE-MATRIX"
      nodesetNames="{ xneg, xpos, yneg, ypos, zneg, zpos }"/>
  </Mesh>

  <NumericalMethods>
    <FiniteElements>
      <FiniteElementSpace
        name="FE1"
        order="1"/>
    </FiniteElements>

    <FiniteVolume>
      <TwoPointFluxApproximation
        name="singlePhaseTPFA"/>
    </FiniteVolume>
  </NumericalMethods>

  <ElementRegions>
    <CellElementRegion
      name="Source"
      cellBlocks="{ 0_hexahedra }"
      materialList="{ water, voidMat }"/>

    <CellElementRegion
      name="Rock"
      cellBlocks="{ 1_hexahedra }"
      materialList="{ water, rockMat }"/>
  </ElementRegions>

  <Constitutive>

    <!-- ================= ROCK ================= -->
    <PorousDamageSpectralElasticIsotropic
      name="rockMat"
      solidModelName="rockSolid"
      porosityModelName="rockPorosity"
      permeabilityModelName="rockPerm"/>

    <!-- E = 40 GPa, nu = 0.23 => K = 24.6914e9, G = 16.2602e9.
         BRITTLE / AT2 (Quadratic dissipation): AT2 puts a real zeroth-order term in the damage
         Jacobian, which the gradient-only AT1 operator lacks. criticalStrainEnergy is unused for
         Brittle and set to zero.
         lengthScale = 0.5 mm gives L/h = 2.1 out to r = 15 mm on this mesh - the minimum for a
         resolved band - then coarsens. A crack running past ~15 mm is mesh-dependent. -->
    <DamageSpectralElasticIsotropic
      name="rockSolid"
      defaultDensity="0.0"
      defaultBulkModulus="24.6914e9"
      defaultShearModulus="16.2602e9"
      lengthScale="0.0005"
      defaultCriticalFractureEnergy="12"
      criticalStrainEnergy="0.0"
      fractureModelType="Brittle"
      localDissipationOption="Quadratic"
      degradationLowerLimit="1e-6"/>

    <BiotPorosity
      name="rockPorosity"
      defaultGrainBulkModulus="128.5e9"
      defaultReferencePorosity="0.05"/>

    <!-- See the header on bulkPermeability. damageDependenceConstant = 12 is carried over from the
         AS deck, where it was set so the fracture out-conducts the radial matrix: the conductance
         ratio at r = 10 mm is 1 at c = 5, 175 at c = 10, 1295 at c = 12. c = 15 is the physically
         natural value but places a 3.3e6 contrast across one cell and diverged in the AS case. -->
    <DamagePermeability
      name="rockPerm"
      bulkPermeability="5.0e-16"
      damageDependenceConstant="12.0"/>

    <!-- ================= BOREHOLE (soft "void") =================
         Same catalog type as the rock, only softer. At 1e-4 x rock stiffness the disc acts as a
         hole while keeping the stiffness matrix conditioned - do not push it to 1e-12. Biot b -> 1
         as K -> 0, so the region transmits pore pressure to the wall as a traction and no
         <Traction> BC is needed. The damage pin keeps it intact. -->
    <PorousDamageSpectralElasticIsotropic
      name="voidMat"
      solidModelName="voidSolid"
      porosityModelName="voidPorosity"
      permeabilityModelName="voidPerm"/>

    <DamageSpectralElasticIsotropic
      name="voidSolid"
      defaultDensity="0.0"
      defaultBulkModulus="24.6914e5"
      defaultShearModulus="16.2602e5"
      lengthScale="0.0005"
      defaultCriticalFractureEnergy="12"
      criticalStrainEnergy="0.0"
      fractureModelType="Brittle"
      localDissipationOption="Quadratic"
      degradationLowerLimit="1e-6"/>

    <BiotPorosity
      name="voidPorosity"
      defaultGrainBulkModulus="128.5e9"
      defaultReferencePorosity="0.99"/>

    <DamagePermeability
      name="voidPerm"
      bulkPermeability="1.0e-10"
      damageDependenceConstant="0.0"/>

    <!-- ================= FLUID =================
         Plain water: rho = 1000, mu = 0.001. No chemical system, no solvent mass. -->
    <CompressibleSinglePhaseFluid
      name="water"
      defaultDensity="1000"
      defaultViscosity="0.001"
      referencePressure="0.0"
      referenceDensity="1000"
      referenceViscosity="0.001"
      compressibility="5e-10"
      viscosibility="0.0"/>

  </Constitutive>

  <Geometry>
    <!-- The two symmetry planes through the borehole axis, used to remove rigid-body motion. The
         nearest off-plane node is 0.056 mm away, so a 0.02 mm box selects the plane and nothing
         else. -->
    <Box
      name="symX"
      xMin="{ -2.0e-5, -0.0751, -0.0006 }"
      xMax="{  2.0e-5,  0.0751,  0.0006 }"/>
    <Box
      name="symY"
      xMin="{ -0.0751, -2.0e-5, -0.0006 }"
      xMax="{  0.0751,  2.0e-5,  0.0006 }"/>

    <!-- Pre-existing notches, left and right of the borehole along x. They give the fracture a
         preferred plane so it propagates from a flaw instead of having to nucleate, and they give
         the damage system a genuine Dirichlet condition - without them it is pure Neumann.
         A SINGLE ROW of nodes on y = 0, not a band: the boxes are 40 um thick and node spacing off
         the plane is 56 um. They run from the borehole wall at 6.35 mm to 8.70 mm (4.7 L), and do
         not share a node with the boreCore pin, which stops at 6.2 mm - the two Dirichlet
         conditions must not touch. -->
    <Box
      name="notchPlus"
      xMin="{  0.00635, -0.00002, -0.0006 }"
      xMax="{  0.00870,  0.00002,  0.0006 }"/>
    <Box
      name="notchMinus"
      xMin="{ -0.00870, -0.00002, -0.0006 }"
      xMax="{ -0.00635,  0.00002,  0.0006 }"/>

    <Cylinder
      name="boreCore"
      firstFaceCenter="{ 0.0, 0.0, -0.001 }"
      secondFaceCenter="{ 0.0, 0.0, 0.001 }"
      outerRadius="0.0062"/>
  </Geometry>

  <FieldSpecifications>

    <!-- ===== initial stress: zero =====
         The 0.15 m lab block is unconfined, so there is no in-situ stress to initialize. Zero also
         avoids starting mechanics and flow out of balance with no stress-initialization task. The
         outer faces are traction-free to match. -->
    <FieldSpecification
      name="Sh_rock"
      initialCondition="1"
      setNames="{ all }"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Rock"
      fieldName="rockSolid_stress"
      component="0"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="SH_rock"
      initialCondition="1"
      setNames="{ all }"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Rock"
      fieldName="rockSolid_stress"
      component="1"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="Sv_rock"
      initialCondition="1"
      setNames="{ all }"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Rock"
      fieldName="rockSolid_stress"
      component="2"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="S_void"
      initialCondition="1"
      setNames="{ all }"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Source"
      fieldName="voidSolid_stress"
      component="0"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <!-- ===== damage: notches seeded at d = 1, borehole pinned at d = 0 ===== -->
    <FieldSpecification
      name="damageNotchInitial"
      initialCondition="1"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="damage"
      scale="1.0"
      setNames="{ notchPlus, notchMinus }"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="damageNotch"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="damage"
      scale="1.0"
      setNames="{ notchPlus, notchMinus }"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="damagePinBore"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="damage"
      scale="0.0"
      setNames="{ boreCore }"/>

    <!-- ===== displacement: traction-free outer faces, plane strain in z =====
         The sample is unconfined, so the side faces get no constraint at all - an absent
         FieldSpecification IS the traction-free condition. In-plane rigid-body modes are removed
         on the symmetry planes through the borehole axis, which add no confining reaction the way
         outer-face rollers would. This imposes mirror symmetry about both axes, so bi-wing planar
         and symmetric four-fold fractures are admissible and asymmetric ones are not. -->
    <FieldSpecification
      name="symX_ux"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="totalDisplacement"
      component="0"
      scale="0.0"
      setNames="{ symX }"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="symY_uy"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="totalDisplacement"
      component="1"
      scale="0.0"
      setNames="{ symY }"/>

    <FieldSpecification
      name="zconstraint"
      objectPath="nodeManager"
      fieldName="totalDisplacement"
      component="2"
      scale="0.0"
      setNames="{ zneg, zpos }"/>

    <!-- ===== flow ===== -->
    <FieldSpecification
      name="initialPressure"
      initialCondition="1"
      setNames="{ all }"
      objectPath="ElementRegions"
      fieldName="pressure"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <!-- Outlet; without it pressure just accumulates. On faceManager, not on cells: a cell-based
         version pins pressure in a band of INTERIOR cells and stops them developing their own
         gradient. The sets are the mesh nodesets, which GEOS propagates to faces (a face joins the
         set when all its nodes do). -->
    <FieldSpecification
      name="outerPressure"
      setNames="{ xneg, xpos, yneg, ypos }"
      objectPath="faceManager"
      fieldName="pressure"
      scale="0.0"/>

    <!-- Mass injection; a negative scale is injection. Do NOT add a pressure Dirichlet on this set
         - Dirichlet is applied before SourceFlux and the two together corrupt the mass row.
         5 mL/min into a 50 mm interval, of which this 1 mm slice takes 1/50, at 1000 kg/m3:
         (5e-6/60)*(1/50)*1000 = 1.6667e-6 kg/s. -->
    <SourceFlux
      name="massInjection"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Source"
      setNames="{ all }"
      scale="-1.6667e-6"/>

  </FieldSpecifications>

  <Tasks>
    <PackCollection
      name="sourcePressureCollection"
      objectPath="ElementRegions/Source/0_hexahedra"
      fieldName="pressure"/>
  </Tasks>

  <Events
    maxTime="200.0">
    <PeriodicEvent
      name="solverApplications"
      maxEventDt="0.5"
      target="/Solvers/pfSolve"/>

    <PeriodicEvent
      name="vtkOutput"
      timeFrequency="1.0"
      target="/Outputs/vtkOutput"/>

    <PeriodicEvent
      name="sourcePressureHistoryCollection"
      timeFrequency="1.0"
      target="/Tasks/sourcePressureCollection"/>
    <PeriodicEvent
      name="sourcePressureHistoryOutput"
      timeFrequency="1.0"
      target="/Outputs/sourcePressureHistoryOutput"/>

    <PeriodicEvent
      name="restarts"
      timeFrequency="50.0"
      target="/Outputs/restartOutput"/>
  </Events>

  <Outputs>
    <VTK
      name="vtkOutput"
      plotLevel="3"/>

    <TimeHistory
      name="sourcePressureHistoryOutput"
      sources="{ /Tasks/sourcePressureCollection }"
      filename="sourcePressureHistory"/>

    <Restart
      name="restartOutput"/>
  </Outputs>

</Problem>
